WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.548 --> 00:00:15.052
We followed Fritz as fast as possible.

2
00:00:15.872 --> 00:00:19.974
But no matter how quickly Jack and I paddled the canoe, we couldn't keep up with the kayak.

3
00:00:19.994 --> 00:00:22.355
"Faster!" cried Ernest.

4
00:00:22.835 --> 00:00:23.895
"We have to catch him!"

5
00:00:24.696 --> 00:00:28.917
We soon arrived outside Safety Bay and passed the rocks that had trapped the wreck.

6
00:00:29.758 --> 00:00:33.059
Although we looked in every direction, we saw no sign of Fritz.

7
00:00:33.839 --> 00:00:35.120
Then we heard a pistol shot.

8
00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:37.020
"Do you think that's Fritz?"

9
00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:38.001
asked Jack.

10
00:00:38.781 --> 00:00:42.122
I fired a shot, which was immediately answered by another shot.

11
00:00:42.822 --> 00:00:45.743
We steered in the direction of the sound and soon saw Fritz.

12
00:00:46.663 --> 00:00:52.124
His kayak darted from behind a point of land, and he paddled over to our canoe, relieved to

13
00:00:52.184 --> 00:00:52.525
see us.

14
00:00:52.545 --> 00:00:56.646
"I didn't intend to pass out of sight, Father," he apologized.

15
00:00:56.666 --> 00:01:00.687
"But once in the water, I was carried along and couldn't help myself.

16
00:01:01.487 --> 00:01:06.221
Then I saw some strange animals that I thought were walruses and—" "Walruses!"

17
00:01:06.501 --> 00:01:07.062
said Ernest.

18
00:01:07.082 --> 00:01:08.823
"I thought they lived in the Arctic."

19
00:01:09.524 --> 00:01:12.346
"I gave chase, but I couldn't catch them—whatever they were."

20
00:01:13.127 --> 00:01:16.029
"You're safe and that is what's important," I said.

21
00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:18.532
I wanted us to all travel together.

22
00:01:19.332 --> 00:01:22.435
But Fritz was eager to go ahead and announce our arrival to Mother.

23
00:01:23.156 --> 00:01:26.078
He was soon skimming over the water while we followed at a slower pace.

24
00:01:27.119 --> 00:01:29.481
Meanwhile black clouds were gathering above us.

25
00:01:30.422 --> 00:01:32.344
Fritz was out of sight and beyond our reach.

26
00:01:33.204 --> 00:01:37.767
We fastened on our life preservers and tied ourselves to the canoe so that we wouldn't be washed

27
00:01:37.827 --> 00:01:41.969
overboard. Fearful gusts of wind lashed the ocean into foam.

28
00:01:42.949 --> 00:01:45.711
Sheets of rain washed over us while lightning flashed.

29
00:01:46.491 --> 00:01:50.153
But the storm swept on its way, and it was over as quickly as it had begun.

30
00:01:51.014 --> 00:01:53.835
Both Ernest and Jack had faced the danger with great courage.

31
00:01:54.656 --> 00:01:55.496
I was proud of them.

32
00:01:56.937 --> 00:02:00.399
I had never lost hope for us, but I feared that Fritz had drowned.

33
00:02:01.179 --> 00:02:03.865
With a heavy heart, I paddled into Safety Bay.

34
00:02:03.885 --> 00:02:06.246
"Look! It's Fritz!"

35
00:02:06.606 --> 00:02:07.226
cried Jack.

36
00:02:08.006 --> 00:02:11.008
There on shore were Fritz, Mother, and little Franz.

37
00:02:11.928 --> 00:02:14.909
Kneeling in prayer, they did not notice us until we shouted.

38
00:02:14.929 --> 00:02:16.710
"You're safe!"

39
00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:17.770
cried Franz.

40
00:02:18.571 --> 00:02:21.372
He ran to greet us as we pulled the canoe onshore.

41
00:02:21.392 --> 00:02:23.253
"Yes," I said.

42
00:02:23.273 --> 00:02:25.613
"Everyone is safe and we are thankful for that."

43
00:02:26.394 --> 00:02:31.456
Returning joyfully to Rockburg, we exchanged our drenched garments for warm, dry clothes.

44
00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:35.121
Seated at a good meal, we talked about the storm.

45
00:02:35.861 --> 00:02:39.002
It was obvious that it had caused a lot of damage at Rockburg.

46
00:02:39.722 --> 00:02:43.103
The next day we discovered that the storm had partly destroyed our bridge.

47
00:02:44.063 --> 00:02:46.704
We were kept busy repairing that for quite some time.

48
00:02:48.284 --> 00:02:49.645
Then it was the fishing season again.

49
00:02:50.625 --> 00:02:54.986
Our storage room was soon stocked with large quantities of herring and sturgeon.

50
00:02:57.027 --> 00:03:01.168
Many quiet days passed after that, and I noticed that my sons were bored.

51
00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:05.742
Fritz proposed that the boys go away for a few days on an expedition.

52
00:03:06.643 --> 00:03:09.965
Ernest, as usual, preferred to remain with my wife and me.

53
00:03:10.725 --> 00:03:15.227
Just as they were ready to start, I noticed Jack slipping some pigeons into a basket.

54
00:03:16.008 --> 00:03:18.629
Then he hid the basket under some packages in the cart.

55
00:03:18.649 --> 00:03:24.172
"Jack is probably afraid the boys won't find anything to eat on their expedition," I said to my

56
00:03:24.233 --> 00:03:26.094
wife. She smiled.

57
00:03:26.534 --> 00:03:30.136
"He might prefer to go hungry rather than eat a tough, old pigeon."

58
00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:33.202
The three boys started off in high spirits.

59
00:03:34.082 --> 00:03:40.387
Storm and Grumble pulled the cart as usual, ridden by Fritz and little Franz, while Jack dashed ahead

60
00:03:40.547 --> 00:03:41.608
on Hurry the ostrich.

61
00:03:42.609 --> 00:03:46.631
That evening we sat on the porch at Rockburg, wondering what the boys were doing.

62
00:03:46.651 --> 00:03:51.655
"We might have news of them soon," said Ernest, looking rather mysterious.

63
00:03:52.556 --> 00:03:54.837
Just then a pigeon landed on the porch railing.

64
00:03:54.857 --> 00:03:57.139
"What is that tied to its leg?"

65
00:03:57.499 --> 00:03:58.140
asked my wife.

66
00:03:58.996 --> 00:04:01.997
Ernest went over to the pigeon and brought back a scrap of paper.

67
00:04:02.017 --> 00:04:06.099
"It's the very latest news," he said, handing me the note.

68
00:04:06.979 --> 00:04:10.021
Dear Parents and Ernest, We have reached Woodlands.

69
00:04:11.041 --> 00:04:13.962
At Wood Lake we captured three young black swans.

70
00:04:14.843 --> 00:04:16.904
Won't they look fine floating on Safety Bay?

71
00:04:17.644 --> 00:04:18.264
Love to all.

72
00:04:18.764 --> 00:04:22.486
Fritz The next morning we sent a message back with the pigeon.

73
00:04:23.346 --> 00:04:27.308
And every evening we received another message describing that day's adventures.

74
00:04:28.292 --> 00:04:33.855
Usually Fritz wrote about the animals they saw, but one night his message was truly alarming.

75
00:04:34.855 --> 00:04:37.897
Dear Parents and Ernest, Today we visited the Gap.

76
00:04:38.697 --> 00:04:41.278
The bamboo fence has been completely broken down!

77
00:04:42.099 --> 00:04:45.300
The fields have been trampled, and we've seen huge footprints.

78
00:04:46.181 --> 00:04:46.981
Come to us, Father.

79
00:04:47.861 --> 00:04:51.623
We are safe right now, but feel we are no match for this unknown danger.

80
00:04:52.384 --> 00:04:52.664
Fritz
